goodix5xx: CLAHE in the image pipeline, and study confirmed presses

Two changes to how well this sensor recognises a finger, both measured on a
27c6:5110 with the same spread enrolment procedure each time.

1. CLAHE between the percentile squash and the unsharp mask.

The global percentile stretch fixes the overall black and white level but
leaves local contrast as the sensor delivered it, and a press sensor delivers
it unevenly: the centre of the contact area is well modulated while the
edges, touched with less pressure, stay washed out. Those edges are what a
displaced press has to share with the template, and FAST finds no corners in
them. 4x4 tiles over the frame with a clip limit, interpolated between
neighbouring tile mappings so no seams appear.

It does not widen the hit zone - a press that lands outside the enrolled area
still has nothing to match - it widens the margin. Match scores went from
16, 7, 13 to 28, 11, 13 against a 7-inlier accept threshold, i.e. the weakest
accepted press moved off the threshold. Keypoints per frame 128 -> 139-144,
extraction 1.7-2.6 ms.

2. Adaptive template study.

The enrolment samples are all the coverage a template will ever have:
libfprint has no way for a driver to amend a print after a verify, so a press
outside the enrolled area keeps failing forever however often the finger is
accepted elsewhere. Confirmed probes are appended to a sidecar file beside
the print, and matching considers them after the enrolled samples.

Three properties keep this from drifting into a skeleton key: only a probe
that matched an *enrolled* sample is learned, never one that only matched
another learned sample, so every stored sample is one hop from the enrolment;
the score has to clear the accept threshold by 3, not merely pass; and at
most 16 samples are kept, oldest out, with the file tied to the enrolment by
a sha256 digest so that re-enrolling discards everything learned about the
old template. The file is 0600 root and written atomically.

Measured in real use after five learned samples: an unlock where every
enrolled sample failed (best 4 of 7 inliers) was carried by a learned sample
at 21 - a finger position the enrolment never covered. A later press at 9
matched but was correctly not learned.

Rejected on measurement while developing these, for the record: lowering
FAST_THRESHOLD from 10 to 7 raises the keypoint count but lowers accuracy
(the extra corners are weak and poorly distinctive, pairing wrongly), and
ORB-style steered BRIEF makes it worse still (the intensity-centroid
orientation is too noisy on low-contrast ridge patches, costing more in
descriptor repeatability than rotation invariance gains).

